<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A sensor produces a hundred updates while an upload consumes ten. Adding coroutines makes the callback return quickly. It also lets unfinished work accumulate. The important question is now where the next update waits, which value may be discarded, and who can see that decision.</p>

Separate demand from overload handling. Suspension or a demand protocol can slow a cooperating producer. Dropping and spilling make different promises; neither turns an uncontrollable source into a slower one.

<!-- @before Internal Mechanics :: guide -->
### Name what must survive before choosing the buffer

A current position can replace an obsolete pending position. A payment instruction cannot disappear merely because a collector is absent. A bounded channel provides an in-process handoff; it does not survive process death. A replay-zero SharedFlow broadcasts to active subscribers and loses emissions when none exist, even with `SUSPEND` configured. Durable work needs persisted intent, a processing owner and an acknowledgement/reconciliation policy.

[Sources: Reactive Streams' non-blocking demand contract](https://www.reactive-streams.org/), [SharedFlow's absent-subscriber behavior](https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.coroutines/kotlinx-coroutines-core/kotlinx.coroutines.flow/-shared-flow/).

